  <h2>One library, many projects</h2>
  <p>
    Store each paper once. Group papers into projects, chapters or workspaces that reference the same copy, so your
    highlights and notes exist in one place no matter where you open the paper.
  </p>

  <h2>Identify papers properly</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Record the <a href={href('glossary/doi/')}>DOI</a> or arXiv ID: it is the one identifier that never changes.</li>
    <li>Fix wrong titles, years and authors once, at import.</li>
    <li>Know whether you have the <a href={href('glossary/preprint/')}>preprint</a> or the published version.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A structure that lasts</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Level</th><th scope="col">What it holds</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Library</td><td>Every paper you've read or plan to</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Project</td><td>The papers for one question, chapter or article</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Notes</td><td>Your thinking, attached to the passage it is about</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Weekly habits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Process new papers the week you add them: a first pass and a one-line note.</li>
    <li>Delete duplicates as soon as you see them.</li>
    <li>Review a project's notes before each writing session.</li>
  </ul>
</GuideLayout>
